---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import AmdahlBar from '../components/diagrams/AmdahlBar.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
import Metric from '../components/ui/Metric.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="The Environment Agents Work In"
  description="Two constraints on what AI agents can do for your developers — tool time and human review — with a diagnostic for the first, what to build, and how to judge maturity."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">The infrastructure argument</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">The Environment Agents Work In</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      Two constraints on what agents can do for your developers, a
      diagnostic for the first, what to build, and how to judge maturity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-12 pt-6">
    <h2>Constraint one: tool time</h2>
    <p class="mt-5">
      At NVIDIA GTC in March 2026, Jeff Dean described a world in which an
      agent operates "50 times faster than a human", and said that the
      start-up time of its tools then becomes "an Amdahl's Law-like
      bottleneck". Make the model infinitely fast, he said, and you get
      "a factor of two or three" reduction in latency, if tools are a
      significant fraction of the work
      (<a href="https://officechai.com/ai/will-need-to-re-engineer-tools-so-that-they-can-keep-up-with-the-speeds-of-ai-agents-googles-jeff-dean/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">as reported by OfficeChai</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      His remark was about latency, and it was conditional. The arithmetic
      behind it is general. Whatever share of an agent's time goes to
      tools, APIs, sign-in flows, and documentation designed for human
      hands is a share that a faster model cannot touch. I have called
      that share the <strong>Amdahl tax</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <AmdahlBar />
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-6">
        <p>
          If 80% of an integration is tool time, the most any model can do
          for the whole is 1.25×. Removing that overhead entirely would be
          worth up to 5×, which is a ceiling and not a forecast: some tool
          time is necessary work, and some is outside the platform's
          control.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>Measuring tool-time share</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        A first reading takes an afternoon:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mt-5 space-y-3 text-body">
        <li>Pick 10 integration tasks developers commonly perform.</li>
        <li>Write the median prompt for each — what a reasonably competent developer would type, not the best prompt.</li>
        <li>Run each prompt through two AI tools.</li>
        <li>For each run, take from the trace the elapsed time spent in model reasoning and in tool calls. Where calls run in parallel, use elapsed time, not the sum of durations.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The ratio is a diagnostic. It shows where an agent's time goes. It
        is not a score, and there is no established benchmark for what
        counts as good.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        A high share does not by itself say where engineering effort pays
        off. Halving tool time saves little on a ten-second task and a
        great deal on a ten-minute one. Prioritize by how much elapsed
        time is avoidable, how often the task is done, and what the fix
        costs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Constraint two: review</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        A person still has to check what the agent produced.
        <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/10/jellyfish-ai-productivity/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Harvard study of engineering data, as reported by InfoQ</a>,
        found that where coding agents were adopted, lines of code rose
        30% and resolved issues 12%, while pull request review time rose
        49% and the share of pull requests needing changes doubled.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Longer reviews do not prove that review is what limits a given
        team. They do suggest a platform can help its developers by making
        agent output easy to verify:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-5 space-y-2">
        <li>a test mode or sandbox where an integration can be exercised safely;</li>
        <li>dry runs that say what a call would do;</li>
        <li>validators for configuration and requests;</li>
        <li>clear success signals, so "it worked" is not a judgment call;</li>
        <li>examples that include their own checks.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-4">
        <h2>What the productivity studies do and don't show</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-10">
        <Metric value="19%" label="Slower with AI tools (measured, early 2025)" deltaDirection="down" />
        <Metric value="20%" label="Faster with AI tools (developers' own estimate)" deltaDirection="up" />
        <Metric value="39 pts" label="Perception vs. reality gap" />
      </div>

      <div class="container-prose px-0">
        <p>
          In <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2025-07-10-early-2025-ai-experienced-os-dev-study/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">METR's randomized controlled trial</a> in early 2025,
          sixteen experienced developers working on their own open-source
          repositories took 19% longer on tasks when AI coding tools were
          allowed, while believing the tools had made them about 20%
          faster.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5">
          <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">METR's February 2026 follow-up</a> points the other way:
          an estimated 18% less time for developers returning from the
          first study and 4% less for new recruits, with intervals that
          include no change. METR says developers are probably more sped
          up now than in early 2025, and that its new data is only very
          weak evidence of how much.
        </p>
        <Callout type="amber" label="What it does and doesn't show">
          The trial did not vary the environment, so it cannot tell us
          that the environment was the constraint. What it shows is that
          developers' sense of how much AI helps them is unreliable. That
          is the case for measuring how agents perform on your platform
          instead of assuming it.
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The Jellyfish data</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        In 2025 <a href="https://jellyfish.co/blog/jellyfish-openai-team-up-to-measure-impact-of-ai-coding-tools/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jellyfish, working with OpenAI</a>, compared
        companies by how regularly their engineers used AI tools. Going
        from 0% to 100% adoption corresponded to an increase from
        <strong>1.36 to 2.90</strong> pull requests per engineer. That is
        an association across companies. It does not say why
        high-adoption companies merge more, and it says nothing about
        their toolchains.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Taken together, the evidence on AI and developer productivity is
        unsettled, and nothing on this page depends on it. The two
        constraints above can be measured on your own platform.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>What to build, in order</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        There are now many ways to publish for agents, and the formats
        keep changing. Work from the most durable to the least, and
        <a href="/measurement/">test each one</a> against its purpose.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mt-5 space-y-4 text-body">
        <li>
          <strong>The product surface.</strong> API design, error messages
          that name the fix, sign-in that works without a browser, a CLI,
          a sandbox. No document compensates for a poor surface.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One source of truth for documentation</strong>, delivered
          as clean markdown as well as HTML, versioned, with deprecations
          marked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The evaluation suite.</strong> Tasks, graders, and
          results. This is where your judgment about what a correct
          integration looks like is kept, and it does not depend on any
          one delivery format.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short, always-available guidance.</strong> A brief index
          or instruction block that agents reliably see. In
          <a href="https://vercel.com/blog/agents-md-outperforms-skills-in-our-agent-evals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel's evaluation</a>, this outperformed an
          on-demand skill.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On-demand packages.</strong> Skills and plugins for
          specific workflows. Check that they load:
          <a href="https://supabase.com/blog/introducing-supabase-evals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supabase found</a> one of its skills loaded in about one
          session in ten until its description was rewritten.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tools.</strong> An MCP server, where an agent needs
          authenticated access to a live system. Watch how much context
          the tool definitions consume.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <Callout type="amber" label="What changed on this page">
        An earlier version ranked platforms by whether they had an MCP
        server, and treated a "recipe library" as the main artifact. An
        MCP server is one item of six, and recipes fold into items 4
        and 5. Maturity is judged by practice, below.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Maturity, by what you know and do</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Level</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Name</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Test</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">0</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Blind</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">No measurement of agent behavior.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">1</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Measured</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">An evaluation suite exists and has been run on more than one model. Agent traffic is visible.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">2</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Served</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agent-facing artifacts exist, each with a stated purpose and a test result.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">3</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Gated</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">The suite runs on releases, and regressions block or flag. Results are tracked by model version. Agent-facing changes get security review.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">4</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Native</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">The product has been changed for agents: sign-in, errors, limits, sandbox, identity, a channel for reporting failures.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>What this means for DevRel's role</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mt-5 space-y-4 text-body">
        <li>
          <strong>Make both constraints visible.</strong> The evaluation
          suite shows where agents lose time and where developers have to
          step in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Build and test the interim artifacts.</strong> Guidance,
          skills, and documentation changes help while deeper platform
          work follows, provided each is shown to help.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Make the investment argument.</strong> Engineering owns
          the surface. DevRel brings traces and failure cases that turn a
          request into evidence.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a href="/measurement/" class="font-semibold">
          Continue to: Measurement →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
